  const story=(id,title,date,city,topic,author,summary,image,body,links=[],related=[])=>REGION.articles[id]={title,date,city,topic,author,summary,image,body,links,related};
  story('fence-followup','A traffic-counting pilot kept more than traffic counts','2026-10-26','Frankfort','Politics','nia-calder','The review identifies a longer retention setting, active demo accounts, and one confirmed misuse. Several questions remain open.','fence-demo',[
    'The Corridor Mobility Board’s camera pilot was approved to count traffic. A published review now describes a route-history function, a retention period twice the promised length, and one employee’s use of the system to check a colleague’s commute.',
    'The 7 October award names Meridian Civil Works as prime contractor and Fence as the sensor subcontractor. The approved scope covers grouped traffic counts and speed bands at three Frankfort-area sites. It does not authorize automated fines or continuous travel histories.',
    'The 23 October memorandum records 37 route-history lookups, a fourteen-day retention setting, and five demonstration accounts that remained enabled. Fence acknowledged one lookup was used to check a colleague’s journey outside the pilot purpose. The record does not establish the motive behind every other lookup.',
    'Fence says it has disabled route queries and removed the demo accounts. That statement is a response to the review, not independent verification of deletion. Collection is paused while the board requests further evidence.',
    'Governor Mandy Schmear has requested a public review. Nothing in the published records establishes that she directed individual tracking. The board’s 27 October meeting will examine the scope, the supplier response, and unanswered access questions.'
  ],[['Read the review memo','#/document/review-memo'],['Original award and limits','#/document/pilot-award'],['Fence response','#/document/fence-response'],['Public hearing','#/document/hearing-agenda']],[ 'camera-demo','hearing-preview','election-calendar']);
  story('camera-demo','Three camera sites, one question-filled demonstration','2026-10-22','Frankfort','Transportation','jules-park','Residents asked what the pilot measures, who can view the records, and whether cameras mean tickets.','vera-demo',[
    'At a Frankfort demonstration, Fence partnerships lead Vera Sloan said the corridor pilot would summarize traffic volumes and speed bands. The public presentation stated that transient records would be deleted after seven days.',
    'Meridian project manager Rowan Pike described the installation schedule. His team coordinates the physical work; Fence manages the platform and the Corridor Mobility Board approves the purpose. The distinction became a recurring question during the evening.',
    'No automated fines are authorized under this contract. Residents also asked about access controls and the possibility of joining vehicle observations into routes. Those questions were submitted for a written response.',
    'Correction, 23 October: an earlier headline described the program as statewide. The award covers three Frankfort-area approach roads. We changed the headline and this description; the pilot is not a statewide rollout. Update, 26 October: a subsequent review identified differences between the public claims and the configuration.'
  ],[['Pilot scope','#/document/pilot-scope'],['Vera Sloan','../linkedup/#/person/vera'],['Follow-up reporting','#/story/fence-followup']],[ 'fence-followup','drains-delay']);
  story('hearing-preview','What Tuesday’s corridor hearing can—and cannot—decide','2026-10-26','Frankfort','Politics','nia-calder','The agenda requests records and public questions. It does not promise an immediate final vote.','capitol-autumn',[
    'The 27 October meeting begins at 5:30 p.m. in the Capitol Learning Room. The Corridor Mobility Board will hear the review findings and Fence’s response before taking resident questions.',
    'Ada Wynn, the public-records director, has published the signed scope, award minutes, review memo, and supplier statement as separate dated documents. Her team publishes the record; it did not award the contract.',
    'Written comments remain open until noon on 28 October. The agenda ends with outstanding records and next steps. Claims that the pilot has already been permanently renewed are not supported by the current packet.'
  ],[['Meeting packet','#/document/hearing-agenda'],['Ada Wynn','../linkedup/#/person/ada']],[ 'fence-followup','reading-room']);
  story('evening-learning','An evening class still needs a way home','2026-10-25','Lexington','Education','morgan-bell','The Governor’s Learning Access proposal has support, practical questions, and no final vote yet.','mandy-schmear-community',[
    'Governor Mandy Schmear’s Learning Access discussion draft would fund evening learning spaces, shared equipment, and transport coordination. The fictional $6.4 million program is proposed, not enacted.',
    'During a learning-center visit, participants asked who would staff the building and how students could get home after the final bus. The draft reserves $800,000 for transport pilots, but committee members can revise that figure.',
    'Riverdale educator Kai Morgan supports the focus on access and wants rural travel included. Librarian Ellis Park says grant forms must be manageable for small organizations. Both describe unanswered implementation questions rather than a finished program.',
    'The next listening session is 29 October at 4 p.m. Public comments will inform a revised draft after the fictional November election.'
  ],[['Read the discussion draft','#/document/learning-access'],['Governor’s official updates','#/capitol?section=governor']],[ 'reading-room','election-calendar']);
  story('election-calendar','November 3: what is on this fictional ballot?','2026-10-25','Frankfort','Politics','nia-calder','Governor Mandy Schmear faces Leon Tusk, alongside assembly and local-board races in the approaching fictional election.','capitol-autumn',[
    'The simulation’s election is Tuesday, 3 November 2026. It includes the governor, assembly, and local-board races. Governor Mandy Schmear is seeking re-election against Leon Tusk.',
    'Her community Frame account includes campaign-season advocacy for Learning Access and links to her campaign account. Advocacy posts are separate from official notices, legislative drafts, and government contact information.',
    'No votes have been cast at the 26 October story snapshot, and a result is not yet known. An opinion poll, a campaign post, and a certified result are different records. This fictional calendar is not real voting guidance.'
  ],[['Official Capitol news','#/capitol?section=news'],['Mandy Schmear on Frame','#/profile/mandy']],[ 'evening-learning','hearing-preview']);
  story('drains-delay','Four more days, three missing drain covers','2026-10-24','Frankfort','Transportation','jules-park','A corridor shoulder project slips to October 30. The contractor’s revised bulletin explains the change.','corridor-roadworks',[
    'Replacement drain covers have moved the Millstone approach completion target from 26 to 30 October. Meridian Civil Works says the closed eastbound shoulder will remain behind barriers until delivery and inspection are complete.',
    'Project manager Rowan Pike walked the signed local alternative after cyclists raised concerns. Bulletin 4 now includes bicycle access through Millstone Lane and the temporary community-hall crossing. One through lane remains open.',
    'Residents have posted photographs of an apparently finished surface. The contractor says the image does not show the unfinished drainage work. The nearby camera pilot is a separate contract; one project’s review does not automatically halt the other.',
    'The location and works in this story are fictional. Public project bulletins inside the simulation provide its timetable.'
  ],[['Current travel bulletin','#/document/road-bulletin'],['Meridian Civil Works','#/site/meridian'],['Resident discussion','#/discussion/commute']],[ 'camera-demo','bus-shelter']);
  story('storm-followup','After the wind: lights restored, leaves still everywhere','2026-10-24','Lexington','Weather','jules-park','A small overnight outage ended before school opened. Cleanup continues around the neighborhood.','org-current-depot',[
    'A fictional overnight windstorm on 23 October interrupted service to 86 Bluegrass Current members in the Elm Hollow area. The cooperative’s morning notice reported all affected connections restored by 7:10 a.m.',
    'The library reopened on schedule, though its courtyard collected enough leaves to qualify as a second collection. Community educator Mara Bell says next month’s tabletop demonstration plans remain unchanged.',
    'The outage was attributed to a fallen branch in the public notice. There is no evidence in the published material of a cyber incident. Photos from a dark street do not establish the cause of an interruption.'
  ],[['Bluegrass Current','#/site/current'],['Company’s classroom plans','../linkedup/#/company/current']],[ 'water-notice','weekend-market']);
  story('water-notice','Low pressure is not the same notice as a boil-water alert','2026-10-26','Frankfort','Utilities','morgan-bell','An old screenshot complicates an ordinary maintenance announcement.','org-lockside-office',[
    'Lockside Water has scheduled valve maintenance in fictional Elm Court for 28 October, 9–11 a.m. Its current notice warns of possible low pressure during the work. It does not announce a boil-water requirement.',
    'A cropped image from an older notice has circulated in a community discussion without its date. Lockside asks residents to compare the full notice and current service area before treating the image as a new announcement.',
    'The provider’s conservation workshops are a different service. Nina Cho’s community team does not schedule repairs or take payments. The new maintenance notice supplies the current service-desk address.'
  ],[['Current maintenance notice','#/document/lockside-notice'],['Discussion and dated clarification','#/discussion/water']],[ 'storm-followup','fiber-help']);
  story('fiber-help','The most popular tech workshop has four chairs','2026-10-23','Louisville','Utilities','morgan-bell','RiverSpan’s small appointments are helping people ask the questions a large presentation can miss.','org-riverspan-open-lab',[
    'RiverSpan Fiber’s community team is trying quieter appointment slots alongside larger workshops. Rafael Cruz says some visitors bring a whole list after discovering they do not have to ask in front of a crowded room.',
    'The format borrows from Open Chair’s quiet-table approach. Planning conversations with Laurel Bend Health concern community learning; they do not establish a contract to operate hospital systems.',
    'Vera Sloan, who previously worked in RiverSpan’s community program, still comments on the practical value of small sessions. A former employee’s comment does not make her current employer a workshop partner.'
  ],[['RiverSpan community site','#/site/riverspan'],['Vera’s work history','../linkedup/#/person/vera']],[ 'reading-room','water-notice']);
  story('reading-room','Frankfort’s next reading group is bringing contracts','2026-10-25','Frankfort','Community','morgan-bell','Capitol Commons plans an evening for people who want to understand the attachments.','commons-reading-room',[
    'Capitol Commons Forum will host a document-reading evening on 29 October at 6 p.m. Guest facilitator Dev Malik says attendees can bring any local issue, from a project bulletin to a library grant proposal.',
    'Ellis Park will bring large-print examples. Ada Wynn has pointed organizers toward the plain-text hearing packet. Their participation does not turn the independent forum into a government office.',
    'The first exercise compares a proposal, an approved contract, and a vendor’s public claim. The second is much more ordinary: finding the version date before arguing about what changed. Tea is promised; matching mugs are not.'
  ],[['Capitol Commons','#/site/capitol-commons'],['Community calendar','#/calendar']],[ 'hearing-preview','evening-learning']);
  story('weekend-market','A makers market where the repair table steals the show','2026-10-24','Lexington','Community','morgan-bell','Pottery, printmaking, and one very patiently repaired toaster fill the autumn planning list.','makers-market',[
    'June Ellis and Nolan Price are among the makers preparing for the 7 November autumn market. Organizer Amira Cole expects ceramics, paper goods, woodwork, and a supervised repair demonstration.',
    'Pressing Matters is supplying a small batch of stall maps. Volunteer Inez Foster asked whether the local-history display could share a rack rather than a separate table. The layout has already gained its third pencil revision.',
    'The event is unrelated to the Community Cup fundraiser. The organizers are still seeking volunteers to carry tables; they are not recruiting a permanent staff.'
  ],[['Pressing Matters','#/site/press'],['Market date','#/calendar']],[ 'new-bakery','library-seeds']);
  story('new-bakery','Second Rise opens with bread, a bench, and no loyalty app','2026-10-22','Louisville','Community','jules-park','A new neighborhood bakery is keeping its first month deliberately small.','second-rise-bakery',[
    'Second Rise Bread opened its fictional Louisville storefront this week. Owners Mina Holt and Ben Arlo are beginning with three loaves, two pastries, and a public bench they hope people will actually sit on.',
    'The shop has no app and no membership tier. Mina says the first operational challenge is making sure the chalkboard is legible before deciding whether anyone needs a digital wallet for a croissant.',
    'Staff at Juniper House shared seating-layout advice before opening. Mina says the bench is already the most-reviewed item the bakery does not sell. The two businesses remain independently owned.'
  ],[['Juniper House','#/site/juniper']],[ 'weekend-market','bus-shelter']);
  story('bus-shelter','The bus shelter argument is really about the last fifty feet','2026-10-21','Louisville','Transportation','jules-park','Residents agree they want a shelter. The accessible approach remains the point of disagreement.','shelter-planning',[
    'At a neighborhood meeting, residents supported a covered stop near the fictional Alder Square shops. They disagreed about whether the proposed paved approach gave wheelchair users enough turning space.',
    'The community board asked for revised drawings before selecting a contractor. Meridian has commented on general construction access but has not been awarded this project. The meeting photograph shows residents reviewing the proposed approach; the shelter has not been built.',
    'A resident’s sketch has been widely shared. It is a suggestion, not an approved construction drawing. The next design session is listed on the community calendar.'
  ],[['Calendar','#/calendar'],['Local discussion','#/discussion/commute']],[ 'drains-delay','evening-learning']);
  story('library-seeds','Seed drawers prepare for a winter of very patient gardening','2026-10-26','Lexington','Community','morgan-bell','The library’s collection includes envelopes, growing notes, and generous uncertainty.','org-library-seed-drawers',[
    'The Lexington Neighborhood Library’s seed swap has acquired a set of reused catalogue drawers. Volunteers are sorting donations by growing season, with a separate section for packets whose variety is uncertain.',
    'Ellis Park asks donors to keep useful notes with the seeds rather than copying a confident description from memory. Camila Ortiz brought envelopes after seeing the community post; that visit does not establish that she works for the library.',
    'A winter repair afternoon and digital-literacy sessions are also being planned. The library’s public site connects those ordinary programs to its growing partner network.'
  ],[['Library','#/site/library'],['Ellis Park','#/profile/ellis']],[ 'reading-room','weekend-market']);
